diagnosis_text:
A full four-phase diagnostic sweep found no fault in this topology. Phase 1 reachability confirmed that the only two end-hosts, pc0 and pc12, exchange traffic with 0% loss in both directions. Phase A infrastructure checks (infra_sweep and l2_snapshot) reported zero flagged devices: every host has an IP and default route, no static ARP poisoning, no duplicate MACs, and no ACL drop rules. Phase B control-plane checks showed a healthy OSPF fabric across the 13 FRR routers, with tc_snapshot reporting no qdisc shaping anywhere. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress processes, CPU spikes, or socket anomalies on any device.

The ospf_snapshot raised flags about r13 and r8, but direct investigation resolved both: r13 is intentionally an EIGRP router (eigrpd confirmed running, ospfd correctly absent), and r8 is a single-router area 2 leaf connected to the backbone via a legitimate virtual link through area 1, with both its Full adjacencies (to r5 and r4 via VLINK0) stable.

Direct inspection of r6 and r7 — the devices named in the monitoring ticket — shows ospfd has been running continuously since lab startup (14:57). Both routers have Full OSPF adjacencies that have been up 5+ minutes with adjacency-formation log entries at 14:58 and no subsequent errors, restarts, or crash records. There is no flap script, no carrier-counter anomaly, and no historical FRR log evidence of a prior crash on either router.

Root cause: No anomaly exists. The ops-chat report of "R6 or R7 crashed" is unconfirmed — the alert that cleared itself has left no mechanism or trace. The network is operating normally.

Fix: No corrective action required; continue monitoring.
